\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Invariants of a selected background}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For a [selected background](fdip-005A), put
#{X=\operatorname{im}P}. The following are invariants of its
[orthogonal comparison class](fdip-005D):}
\ul{
\li{the spectrum of #{Q}, with multiplicities, and the rank of #{P};}
\li{the spectrum of #{S_P}, hence its determinant and trace;}
\li{for each eigenvalue #{\lambda} of #{Q}, the principal angles
between #{X} and #{E_\lambda=\ker(Q-\lambda I)}.}
}
\p{Here principal angles are defined by taking orthonormal inclusion
matrices #{B_X,B_\lambda} and the
#{\min(\dim X,\dim E_\lambda)} singular values of
#{B_X^*B_\lambda}. These numbers lie in #{[0,1]}; their arccosines,
in increasing order, are the angles. This definition includes zero
singular values and uses the entire eigenspace when #{\lambda}
is repeated. It is independent of the two orthonormal bases.}

\proof{
\p{If #{Q'=OQO^{-1}} and #{P'=OPO^{-1}}, then
#{O(X)=X'} and #{O(E_\lambda)=E'_\lambda}. Choosing the transported
bases leaves #{B_X^*B_\lambda} unchanged; changing either basis
multiplies this matrix on the left or right by an orthogonal matrix,
which does not change its singular values. These are at most one
because both inclusions and orthogonal projections have operator
norm at most one. Conjugation gives the spectrum of #{Q} and
rank of #{P}. The [selected-law transport](fdip-005C) gives
#{S_{P'}=(O|_X)S_P(O|_X)^{-1}}, proving the remaining claims.}
}

\p{These are consequently invariants of both graph groupoids
#{\mathsf{Bg}} and #{\mathsf{Bg}^{\pm}}. There is additional
incidence information: the isomorphism class of the weighted
incidence presentation with its selector, and the order of its
finite automorphism group, are invariants in the chosen graph
groupoid. Indeed, an arrow #{g:b\to b'} induces the group
isomorphism #{\alpha\mapsto g\alpha g^{-1}} of automorphism groups.
The directed and signed groups are different choices here;
#{|\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathsf{Bg}}(b)|} is not being asserted
invariant under an arbitrary signed arrow.}

\p{The [cospectral pair](fdip-005H) has an orthogonal comparison
but no graph arrow. Thus even all the listed spectral and angle
invariants cannot recover incidence. No completeness claim for
invariants of the pair #{(Q,P)} under #{O(H)} is made.}

\p{TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.cosPrincipalAngles} defines
the singular values of the overlap operator of two finite
orthonormal families as a nonincreasing sequence padded by zeros.
Taking its first #{\min(\dim X,\dim E_\lambda)} values and then
arccosines gives the convention above. Its
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.cosPrincipalAngles_le_one} supplies the
upper bound; the background-conjugacy and incidence claims are
the separate arguments here.}
